---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site.js";
import "../styles/pages.css";
---
<Base
  title={`About · ${SITE.name}`}
  description={`Why ${SITE.name} exists: India publishes a staggering amount of data and hides most of it on websites that feel like punishment. This site breaks it out and puts it in one place.`}
  nav="about"
  dateStamp="October 2026"
  crumbLeft="<b>About</b>"
  crumbRight="Why this exists, and how to reach me"
  canonical="/about/"
>
<div class="page spage">

  <div class="titleblock">
    <div class="kicker">About</div>
    <h1>Why this exists</h1>
    <p class="lede">India publishes a staggering amount of data and hides most of it on websites that feel like punishment. This site breaks it out and puts it in one place.</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>A frustration</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>I write <a href={SITE.parent.url}>{SITE.parent.name}</a> to explain India through government data, and getting at that data is a pain in the ass. Most of it sits on websites last updated around the time Moses parted the Red Sea, built, it seems, to teach Indians patience rather than answer their questions.</p>
    <p>Using them feels like penance for sins committed in a previous life. I can find no other explanation for how bad they are.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>More than you think</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>For all the talk of India being data-starved, and of its official numbers being untrustworthy, the government publishes an enormous amount: on the economy, money, farms, health, society and culture. Most Indians have no idea it exists.</p>
    <p>Academics have built up an immunity to the portals; everyone else gives up. As for journalists, there are about three in this country. The rest write in a way that makes ChatGPT and Claude look like Hemingway and Shakespeare.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>A sister project</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p><a href={SITE.parent.url}>{SITE.parent.name}</a> explains life in India through this data, tied to how people actually live rather than to abstract mental, linguistic and semantic ejaculations about a data set. {SITE.name} is its sister project: it breaks the data out of those portals and puts it in one place. The explaining happens on {SITE.parent.name}.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Where this is going</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>It is slow work, because these data sets come with no shortage of problems: base years that change, series that stop, tables that disagree with the printed report. How I handle them is in <a href="/method/">the method</a>.</p>
    <p>The goal is for this to become the place to discover every data set the government publishes, wherever it is published.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>Get in touch</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>Ideas, feedback, suggestions, or want to work on this together? Write to me.</p>
    <ul class="ab-contact">
      <li><span>Email</span><a href="mailto:bhuvan@bebhuvan.com">bhuvan@bebhuvan.com</a></li>
      <li><span>X</span><a href="https://twitter.com/bebhuvan">@bebhuvan</a></li>
      <li><span>LinkedIn</span><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/bebhuvan/">in/bebhuvan</a></li>
      <li><span>GitHub</span><a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repo.replace("https://github.com/", "")}</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>If a number here differs from its source, tell me which chart and I will check it against the release. <a href="/contribute/">How to contribute</a> covers reporting it, suggesting a dataset and working on the code.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>How it's built</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <p>{SITE.name} is a static website: no trackers, no cookies, no accounts. Visits are counted with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookie and does not follow anyone across sites. Data is fetched from the statistical agencies by a scheduled pipeline, checked, and built into the pages; charts are drawn when the site is built and download as an image or as data, with the source attached. The text is set in Newsreader and Libre Franklin.</p>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>
